1. a refinement is a Module holding Refinement objects
   Shout.class                                    Module
   Shout.refinements                              [#<refinement:String@Shout>]
   Shout.refinements.first.class                  Refinement
   Shout.refinements.first.target                 String
   Refinement.superclass                          Module
2. before using: String is untouched
   "hi".shout                                     NoMethodError
   "hi".respond_to?(:shout)                       false
   String.method_defined?(:shout)                 false
3. after using: the rest of this file sees it
   "hi".shout                                     HI!
   "hi".length  (an existing method, replaced)    refined length
   ["a", "b"].map(&:shout)                        ["A!", "B!"]
   after_using  (a method written below using)    HI!
4. the scope is lexical: where code is written, not when it runs
   before_using  (written above the using line)   NoMethodError
   a method in a file required after using        NoMethodError
   eval('"z".shout')  (a string eval'd here)      Z!
   using inside a method                          RuntimeError: main.using is permitted only at toplevel
5. what reflection sees inside the scope
   "hi".respond_to?(:shout)                       true
   "hi".send(:shout)                              HI!
   "hi".public_send(:shout)                       HI!
   "hi".method(:shout).owner                      #<refinement:String@Shout>
   String.instance_methods.include?(:shout)       false
   String.method_defined?(:shout)                 false
   Module.used_modules                            [Shout]
   Module.used_refinements                        [#<refinement:String@Shout>]
6. super reaches the original; a refinement sees only what was active where it was written
   "hi".upcase  (refined; calls super)            politely HI
   "hi".shout  (its upcase is still the original) HI!
   Module.used_modules                            [Politely, Shout]
